{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/72757-since-the-aeoniverse-is-a-generational-game","id":72757,"title":"Since the Aeoniverse is a generational game...","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-02-15T12:05:50-08:00","id":72757,"title":"Since the Aeoniverse is a generational game...","content":"I am not sure if anyone here is familiar with a gaming system called Amazing Engine or not. In short, the players would make a character core. Say, one player wanted his characters to be more physical and another player wanted her characters to be more social? They would then use that character core to help them build their characters in the various games (all the way from an Aliens-esque game to one that incorporated magic and science together to another one where you investigated the stuff put into tabloids). When you earned XP, you could then use it to help improve that character or else channel it to the character core to help in future character creation. <br /> <br /> Now, with that being said, what about translating this into something for Aeoniverse? Why not allow a player core since the game lends itself so well to prioritization of Physical, Mental and Social? Then you can develop the core and use it to make your characters from there? Points funneled to the character core can then be used to enhance the characters in the next game? <br /> <br /> For example, I have Michael. I want him to be primarily Physical. I would assign that as the number one priority. I want him to be also quick on his mental feet so I make Mental secondary and this leaves Social as tertiary. This means that in all characters I make for all three settings, then the character will have those points allocated. Now, Michael is made in Trinity. I make him a Legionnaire. He squishes Aberrants and then burns the corpses for good measure. I also want him to dabble in Biokinesis because..well..it is good to look at an Aberrant and cause his head to cave in but sometimes, you just wanna run up to them and stick your fist through their chest...or whatever they have.<br /> <br /> Now, I earn some XP as Michael and then I use half of it to put into the character core. That is where I am stuck. Should X amount of XP lower the cost of Physical? I think dots are way out unless the cost was astronomical like 20Xp/dot in future character. But what about a discount on using Freebies to improve the Attributes? What are your ideas on such an endeavor?<br /> <br /> BTW, I am playing around with this idea since I plan on running my players in an Aeoniverse 2nd edition game. They will start out in Trinity and then play some novas with links to the Psions and then lastly play some Inspire who are related to either the Psions or the Novas. And they will shift between characters per session when dramatically appropriate such as revealing some detail o fact that the players would need to know to help out one of their characters in another time. Or even a flashback such as reading a journal/blog of one of the characters from the previous time. Has anyone tried such a task and if so, how did it go for them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-16T12:43:03-08:00","id":73308,"title":"","content":"This sounds like an interesting house rule, but it probably wouldn't be added to the core mechanics. It kinda reminds me of something I read in a Knight of the Dinner Table comic, wherein the players had protege's setup in case of their inevitable deaths.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/765-darksol-aeternium","name":"Darksol-aeternium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar765_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"392"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-16T12:47:31-08:00","id":73313,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>thedonnie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/72757-since-the-aeoniverse-is-a-generational-game#post72757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, with that being said, what about translating this into something for Aeoniverse? Why not allow a player core since the game lends itself so well to prioritization of Physical, Mental and Social? Then you can develop the core and use it to make your characters from there? Points funneled to the character core can then be used to enhance the characters in the next game?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if a major sub-system is precisely the way to go, but the question of collaboration, fore/postshadowing and the idea of Trinity as a temporal continuum as well as a genre one is absolutely going to be addressed. A set of guidelines and optional rules wouldn't be out of the question.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Neall Raemonn Price</span><br /> Beleaguered Scion Developer</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/137-neall","name":"Neall","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar137_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1204"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-16T13:59:59-08:00","id":73350,"title":"","content":"I apologize if anyone thought that this was something to be included. I was wondering if anyone had any thoughts about how to implement something like that as a houserule. My group is all about the houserules. LOL. I was hoping to see if anyone had any suggestions about how to get it done, like how to convert any Xp to a character core and such.<br /> <br /> See, I love epic sagas and the Aeoniverse is truly perfect for that. Moreso than any other line. Scion will have your characters go from nobodies to deities in a matter of a handful of years. Exalted can also have the same thing done in a few years. The WoD (c or n) can do it but only for certain splats. But with Aeoniverse, it is built in.<br /> <br /> So I wanted to try this out at my table and see if anyone had any suggestions to help me implement the general idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-22T11:06:50-08:00","id":77209,"title":"","content":"I am familiar with the Amazing Engine system, I ran a few intertwined games in it. The campaign was kind of like &quot;Quantum Leap&quot; mashed together with Andre Norton's &quot;Crosstime&quot; novels (and which had a somewhat psychedelic quality very similar to &quot;Life on Mars&quot; too though the show was not out back then) where the characters were interdimentional &quot;cops&quot; who did not physically travel between between parallel dimensions but projected into compatible hosts instead.<br /> <br /> The complication was that the station the characters were based at fell to attackers long ago and everyone was dead, including the PC's original bodies which caused a trauma to the agents in the field. By the time the AI came back up and got the station into minimal functionality the PC's (who had not had a chance to link up with each other before the &quot;comms&quot; went down) each thought that it was a realistic fantasy they once had.<br /> <br /> The AI could not bring them back into the station but it could do a remote-to-remote transfer and implant a small amount of information in the process so all of a sudden the crotchety old geezers suddenly found themselves young again but strangely different and in a very weird world (20th century earth to the bizarrely retrofuturistic world of &quot;Once and Future King&quot; in the first story) with a few clues as to who the other team members were and an itch to find them and.... fix something (they had to work out what the mission was supposed to be from clues too). Even after they got together there was still a small nagging doubt about if it was real or just another psychotic break from reality coming back to haunt them.<br /> <br /> Anyway, to get back on subject:<br /> <br /> The core system was a good idea; in theory one could make a kind of sketchy character once (the core) and it would plug into the various worlds templates with little effort needed for making the shell; the character (or character family) would then each progress more or less in sync with each other and any new ones would be made with benefits from previous play with the others integrated automatically. The templates even had enough wiggle room to make fairly major adjustments (in the case of my campaign we used it to account for different host capabilities) and still keep the build reasonably aligned with the original.<br /> <br /> In practice it did not quite work that smoothly since the various worlds did not fit together that well with each introducing extensions to the basic system without enough regard to compatibility. It was more like making entirely separate characters with a similar theme and some small experience adjustment from the others and simply saying they are the same character. In the case of my campaign that made it impractical to switch hosts each story in the series like I originally intended and we ended up using the same &quot;hosts&quot; for several stories and ignored the problem of what happened to the hosts between &quot;activations&quot; when they came back from another parellel. It had potential but like a lot of TSR games it had some serious glitches with the overall system engine.<br /> <br /> From what I have heard in this forum it sounds like the new Aeonverse system is going to have a kind of core and shell structure itself so the house rule stuff may be a matter of keeping track of experience between instances of the character and assign it to traits that would be considered &quot;core&quot; and those that would be in essence &quot;shell&quot; and copy the &quot;core&quot; upgrades to the derivative &quot;local&quot; copies. Since it is all a single &quot;world&quot; at different points in time there would not be a need for all the rules AE had for taking the character through what were essentially different games with only a limited resemblance to each other system wise.<br /> <br /> Also, doing crossovers in the current Aeon rule-set is not as smooth as it should be but it is still easier than shelling AE cores and definitely easier than transplanting AE characters and equipment whole between the games in that set. Aberrant/Trinity crossovers for instance are a bit more complex than, say, a combined Vampire/Mage/Werewolf game but not that much more.<br /> <br /> You would probably be better off making parallel characters normally, keeping the style of the character in mind, and taking the experience (or a percentage of it) each session and adding it to each instance of the character and then applying it using the normal rules. The storyteller system and the derivatives of it that I am familliar with are more flexible and less crunchy than the AE system(s) so there is less need for fitting big chunks of clunky crunch together so the problem AE was set up to solve is not such a problem in it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":869,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/869-bc001","user":"BC001","date":". 02-22-2014, 02:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/869-bc001","name":"BC001","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar869_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}